1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,000
Buckle up and hold your breath.

2
00:00:02,001 --> 00:00:05,000
You've just detoured into a world of peril.

3
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Danger waits around every hairpin turn, high in the Andes.

4
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:12,000
There is no room for mistakes.

5
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:15,000
It is completely unforgiving.

6
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:18,000
And on the deadliest six-mile ribbon of asphalt on Earth.

7
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Beware the fog and avalanches.

8
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:26,000
There's really no second chances up here.

9
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:29,000
But if it thrills your Raptor, punch that accelerator.

10
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,200
Trust me, there's a ton of adrenaline going through your body.

11
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:34,860
It's too late for a U-turn.

12
00:00:35,140 --> 00:00:39,560
Now you're traveling the world's most dangerous drives on Modern Marvels.

13
00:00:55,640 --> 00:00:59,420
A grim reality exists every time you get behind the wheel.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,540
any road can suddenly become deadly.

15
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:08,780
The next drive you take could be your last,

16
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:14,100
whether it's road design, the elements, or other drivers.

17
00:01:15,780 --> 00:01:18,900
All it takes is one false move.

18
00:01:19,820 --> 00:01:22,260
One of the most dangerous roads of all,

19
00:01:22,360 --> 00:01:24,760
according to many experts, lies in Bolivia,

20
00:01:25,380 --> 00:01:28,260
part of a highway system linking Bolivia's capital,

21
00:01:28,261 --> 00:01:30,060
La Paz and Coroico.

22
00:01:30,720 --> 00:01:32,320
It snakes 35 miles

23
00:01:32,321 --> 00:01:33,940
through the towering Andes Mountains.

24
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:36,920
Its official name

25
00:01:36,921 --> 00:01:38,760
is the North Yungas Road.

26
00:01:39,380 --> 00:01:41,180
But those who dare to drive it,

27
00:01:41,580 --> 00:01:43,500
as well as those too fearful to try,

28
00:01:44,340 --> 00:01:45,040
call it

29
00:01:45,041 --> 00:01:46,380
El Camino de la Muerte,

30
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:48,200
the Death Road.

31
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:54,460
Along this narrow strip of dirt,

32
00:01:54,461 --> 00:01:56,100
perched over vertical drops

33
00:01:56,101 --> 00:01:57,760
of nearly 2,000 feet,

34
00:01:58,260 --> 00:02:07,020
Between 200 and 300 motorists perish each year.

35
00:02:11,020 --> 00:02:14,300
Wilkang Ziegler has driven the death road many times.

36
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:22,940
The road is as unforgiving of any road that I've ever driven on in the world.

37
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:29,180
The road itself is probably the width of an SUV.

38
00:02:30,240 --> 00:02:33,040
And you've got vehicles trying to pass each other.

39
00:02:36,280 --> 00:02:40,920
And the vast majority of the accidents are collisions

40
00:02:40,921 --> 00:02:44,140
where one of the vehicles or both of the vehicles

41
00:02:44,141 --> 00:02:49,580
literally fall off of the sheer cliff that make up the road.

42
00:02:49,581 --> 00:02:57,100
Since it opened in the 1930s, the death road has claimed thousands of lives, and many fatalities

43
00:02:57,101 --> 00:02:58,860
beyond that have gone unreported.

44
00:03:00,820 --> 00:03:06,160
Many of the road's victims have plunged to their deaths during the winter months, when

45
00:03:06,161 --> 00:03:15,040
rushing water, falling rocks, and fall make driving conditions even more treacherous.

46
00:03:15,041 --> 00:03:23,960
In the rainy season, which is November through March, the road basically turns to slime.

47
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:28,380
There's a lot of vehicles that slip off the side of the road in the rain.

48
00:03:34,520 --> 00:03:40,260
The best bet to survive this treacherous death trap is choosing the right vehicle to navigate it.

49
00:03:40,261 --> 00:03:45,580
You need a four-wheel drive vehicle and something that has excellent traction.

50
00:03:46,720 --> 00:03:51,260
Be ready for quick maneuvers without over-controlling.

51
00:03:51,620 --> 00:03:55,440
Over-controlling with no room for error is going to get you killed.

52
00:03:55,860 --> 00:03:57,180
There is no room for mistakes.

53
00:03:57,360 --> 00:04:01,220
It's completely unforgiving of any carelessness or neglect.

54
00:04:03,440 --> 00:04:09,540
In 2006, the Bolivian government completed this modern, safer road to be an alternative.

55
00:04:10,260 --> 00:04:13,580
But hundreds still die every year along the old route,

56
00:04:14,020 --> 00:04:15,620
as some truckers still drive here,

57
00:04:16,540 --> 00:04:20,340
including those that deliver goods to towns that have grown up along the road.

58
00:04:22,940 --> 00:04:27,100
A lot of these truck drivers have made their living on this road,

59
00:04:27,220 --> 00:04:34,640
and they get paid by how many times they make this 35-mile trip back and forth.

60
00:04:34,641 --> 00:04:39,780
and they now share the peril with thrill-seeking tourists

61
00:04:39,781 --> 00:04:43,340
that flock here for the adrenaline rush of the ultimate driving test.

62
00:04:44,120 --> 00:04:48,460
It's a world landmark when you travel to Bolivia.

63
00:04:49,280 --> 00:04:53,960
It's like the climbing of Everest when you go to Nepal.

64
00:04:53,961 --> 00:04:58,600
It's bragging rights of having driven on the world's most dangerous road.

65
00:05:00,300 --> 00:05:03,020
Thrill-seeking mountain bikers from around the world

66
00:05:03,021 --> 00:05:04,620
covet those bragging rights too.

67
00:05:04,640 --> 00:05:09,260
More than 25,000 have taken the dare.

68
00:05:12,600 --> 00:05:15,360
However, even cycling is not without risk.

69
00:05:16,340 --> 00:05:19,660
Nine people were killed when an SUV hit a cyclist,

70
00:05:19,860 --> 00:05:21,820
and both plummeted 300 feet.

71
00:05:22,620 --> 00:05:23,720
There were no survivors.

72
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:29,160
But a road doesn't need blind curves or hairpin turns to be deadly.

73
00:05:29,161 --> 00:05:37,340
Another of the world's most dangerous roads lies 8,000 miles from Bolivia in the war-torn

74
00:05:37,341 --> 00:05:39,540
nation of Iraq.

75
00:05:39,541 --> 00:05:44,280
It's the stretch of road from central Baghdad that leads seven miles to the country's main

76
00:05:44,281 --> 00:05:49,740
airport.

77
00:05:49,741 --> 00:05:55,440
It's a chilling reminder that danger can also lurk on a flat paved road with no sharp turns

78
00:05:55,441 --> 00:05:57,460
or precipitous cliffs.

79
00:05:57,461 --> 00:06:02,040
During the Iraq war, a trip on this short span of roadways seemed like the longest drive

80
00:06:02,041 --> 00:06:03,980
of your life.

81
00:06:03,981 --> 00:06:09,300
As a critical traffic artery in and out of Baghdad for both soldiers and civilians, it

82
00:06:09,301 --> 00:06:15,760
was a prime target for insurgents.

83
00:06:15,761 --> 00:06:17,580
Their weapons of choice?

84
00:06:17,581 --> 00:06:18,580
IEDs.

85
00:06:18,581 --> 00:06:28,280
an IED is an improvised explosive device that's where the threat takes almost anything that they

86
00:06:28,281 --> 00:06:34,020
have available such as leftover artillery munitions leftover mortar bombs or even just

87
00:06:34,021 --> 00:06:39,240
packages of explosives they'll place them at the side of the road have some kind of initiation

88
00:06:39,241 --> 00:06:43,400
device and they'll launch that at a target when they see fit

89
00:06:43,401 --> 00:06:51,360
IED attacks on the road to Baghdad Airport happened so often that the road was nicknamed

90
00:06:51,361 --> 00:06:52,660
IED Alley.

91
00:06:54,600 --> 00:06:58,260
To help make driving down this dangerous road safer, the U.S.

92
00:06:58,261 --> 00:06:59,820
Army turned to its experts

93
00:06:59,821 --> 00:07:04,260
in Warren, Michigan, at the Tank Automotive Research Development and Engineering Center,

94
00:07:04,860 --> 00:07:05,560
or TARDEC.

95
00:07:06,700 --> 00:07:11,280
They spent several years developing armor for Humvees and other vehicles traversing the

96
00:07:11,281 --> 00:07:11,981
road.

97
00:07:13,400 --> 00:07:17,920
This essential technology was developed to protect troops during the Iraq War.

98
00:07:18,640 --> 00:07:21,280
The first phase was add-on armor.

99
00:07:21,820 --> 00:07:27,860
We had to provide armor protection, not only just the metal and ballistic metals on there,

100
00:07:27,980 --> 00:07:31,540
but we couldn't completely close the vehicle up because folks need to operate,

101
00:07:31,780 --> 00:07:35,620
so we had to also develop and integrate ballistic glass as well

102
00:07:35,621 --> 00:07:39,840
to protect our warfighters against IEDs, small arms fire,

103
00:07:39,920 --> 00:07:41,780
and those things that they had experienced on the battlefield.

104
00:07:41,781 --> 00:07:45,220
Initially, we could just put a piece of steel up here.

105
00:07:45,221 --> 00:07:47,560
But as we needed to go to lighter weight,

106
00:07:47,561 --> 00:07:52,720
we'd go to an S2 glass, fiberglass, combined with a Kevlar.

107
00:07:52,721 --> 00:07:54,900
And we actually used, for even higher performance

108
00:07:54,901 --> 00:07:58,460
and lighter weight, ceramics.

109
00:07:58,461 --> 00:08:01,540
For reasons of security, TARDEC engineers

110
00:08:01,541 --> 00:08:03,900
won't go into more detail about the composition

111
00:08:03,901 --> 00:08:06,540
of its innovative armors or the types of projectiles

112
00:08:06,541 --> 00:08:07,940
it uses to test them.

113
00:08:11,780 --> 00:08:17,860
We have a number of different types of rounds, both bullets and fragments that we shoot,

114
00:08:18,380 --> 00:08:24,780
to see how well an armor design performs against those particular projectiles.

115
00:08:25,260 --> 00:08:28,520
We're looking to balance three things. It's called the three Ps.

116
00:08:29,060 --> 00:08:31,580
Payload, protection, and performance.

117
00:08:32,300 --> 00:08:38,240
So as we add protection to a vehicle system, that added weight reduces the payload,

118
00:08:38,241 --> 00:08:40,880
the carrying capacity of that vehicle system.

119
00:08:40,881 --> 00:08:45,400
it also negatively impacts the automotive performance of the vehicle.

120
00:08:45,920 --> 00:08:50,180
So what we do here at TARDAC is the engineering and analysis

121
00:08:50,181 --> 00:08:53,620
to determine how do you trade off those three things.

122
00:08:58,460 --> 00:09:03,600
For years, one of the most dangerous roads in the world was Baghdad's Airport Road,

123
00:09:04,900 --> 00:09:10,540
referred to as Death Road by locals, known as IED Alley to U.S. military.

124
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:17,720
A key part of the strategy to protect soldiers was to neutralize the threat of the explosive

125
00:09:17,721 --> 00:09:21,080
devices themselves.

126
00:09:21,081 --> 00:09:22,840
The first step?

127
00:09:22,841 --> 00:09:28,220
Deploy troops in a convoy of vehicles to run a route clearance operation.

128
00:09:28,221 --> 00:09:29,820
Their mission?

129
00:09:29,821 --> 00:09:38,500
Find roadside IEDs before they explode.

130
00:09:38,501 --> 00:09:43,900
On this day, soldiers were training for hazardous duty at Port Leonard Wood in Missouri.

131
00:09:43,901 --> 00:09:47,040
This platform that I'm sitting on here is what we call the RG-31.

132
00:09:47,041 --> 00:09:51,300
We use this platform as front and rear security to our route clearance formations.

133
00:09:51,301 --> 00:09:56,140
There are some technologies that we've developed to enhance this particular platform formation.

134
00:09:56,141 --> 00:10:00,180
One of those technologies is the mine roller, and that sits out in front of the vehicle,

135
00:10:00,181 --> 00:10:05,540
and anything that is subsurface or buried, an IED perhaps, or a mine, if it's going to

136
00:10:05,541 --> 00:10:09,380
explode, we want to explode it with that roller.

137
00:10:09,381 --> 00:10:12,880
The RG-31 also sports a powerful camera,

138
00:10:12,881 --> 00:10:15,180
allowing soldiers to identify potential threats

139
00:10:15,181 --> 00:10:17,260
from a distance.

140
00:10:17,261 --> 00:10:18,380
How far?

141
00:10:18,381 --> 00:10:19,081
They won't say.

142
00:10:21,820 --> 00:10:24,060
And the route clearance convoy is bolstered

143
00:10:24,061 --> 00:10:27,060
by this battle-ready bad boy.

144
00:10:27,061 --> 00:10:28,800
Behind me, what you're looking at right now

145
00:10:28,801 --> 00:10:31,860
is what we call the Buffalo.

146
00:10:31,861 --> 00:10:34,860
Taller than a tank and fitted with large armored

147
00:10:34,861 --> 00:10:41,960
glass windows, the Buffalo offers soldiers inside a wide and strategic range of view.

148
00:10:41,961 --> 00:10:48,180
It also features a robotic arm with a pitchfork-like hand, capable of extending to remove suspicious

149
00:10:48,181 --> 00:10:56,240
objects from a road surface or for digging up IEDs buried beneath a road.

150
00:10:56,241 --> 00:11:00,980
There are several types of IEDs. Some are radio controlled, some are command wired.

151
00:11:00,981 --> 00:11:04,480
They have to be aware of all of that so that as they come up on something,

152
00:11:04,600 --> 00:11:06,500
they can identify it pretty quickly and easily,

153
00:11:06,660 --> 00:11:09,060
get it out of there before somebody's injured by it.

154
00:11:10,980 --> 00:11:15,020
This team in Iraq discovered an IED just buried off the roadside.

155
00:11:15,800 --> 00:11:19,220
If you don't see any wires, it could still be around in there, say it's remote.

156
00:11:20,300 --> 00:11:22,220
The team successfully unearthed it.

157
00:11:22,540 --> 00:11:24,980
We got around. There you go. We got around.

158
00:11:25,980 --> 00:11:26,680
Awesome.

159
00:11:26,681 --> 00:11:32,860
Soldiers manning the Buffalo can also deploy a remote-controlled robot.

160
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,940
I can't see inside a culvert, so I'll unload this one,

161
00:11:36,100 --> 00:11:40,060
I'll run it down into the ditch, look into a culvert.

162
00:11:40,061 --> 00:11:44,200
If I have a suspected item, then I can deal with it.

163
00:11:45,080 --> 00:11:46,900
If there's nothing there, great.

164
00:11:47,020 --> 00:11:49,740
I put the robot back on the chassis and we continue our mission.

165
00:11:49,741 --> 00:11:57,740
However, clearing every IED from the road to Baghdad airport and other roads in Iraq is impossible.

166
00:11:57,741 --> 00:12:03,740
And when they would go off, the dangers went far beyond the explosions.

167
00:12:03,741 --> 00:12:09,740
IEDs can create a gauntlet of sizable craters that is difficult and dangerous to navigate.

168
00:12:09,741 --> 00:12:13,740
The Army's answer was a concrete mobile mixer.

169
00:12:13,741 --> 00:12:17,740
The next best thing to a concrete plant on wheels.

170
00:12:17,741 --> 00:12:25,740
Soldiers from the Army's 230th Concrete Team move fast to complete the daunting task called rapid crater repair.

171
00:12:25,741 --> 00:12:32,740
A typical civilian ready-mix truck has to report to a batch plant to receive its load to go out and perform its mission.

172
00:12:32,741 --> 00:12:42,740
One of the advantages of the concrete mobile mixer over a typical ready-mix truck is that the aggregate and the Portland cement remain on the vehicle drive.

173
00:12:42,741 --> 00:12:50,740
Equipped with water tanks, the mobile mixer can easily whip up a batch of concrete at the mission site in a matter of minutes.

174
00:12:52,740 --> 00:12:55,740
Then, crater repair begins.

175
00:12:56,740 --> 00:13:00,740
It's a race against the clock under threat of ambushes and enemy sniper fire.

176
00:13:02,740 --> 00:13:08,740
Soldiers finish the job by signing the new section of the roadway with their nickname, the Goons.

177
00:13:08,741 --> 00:13:13,460
Then it's off to the next crater.

178
00:13:15,740 --> 00:13:20,620
Even if you aren't driving in a war zone, danger of another kind may await.

179
00:13:21,420 --> 00:13:26,200
Because when Mother Nature has her way, all bets are off.

180
00:13:27,580 --> 00:13:32,480
One variable can turn even the most innocuous stretch of road into a death trap.

181
00:13:36,280 --> 00:13:37,280
The weather.

182
00:13:38,740 --> 00:13:44,740
On average, more than six million vehicle crashes occur in the United States each year,

183
00:13:44,741 --> 00:13:48,740
and one quarter are attributed to weather.

184
00:13:52,740 --> 00:13:56,740
Statistically, the deadliest kind of weather to drive in is fog.

185
00:13:56,741 --> 00:14:03,740
And driving in it could feel like the most dangerous drive of all, one with zero visibility.

186
00:14:03,741 --> 00:14:10,380
In this satellite photo, that's not snow draping over 400 miles of California's Central Valley.

187
00:14:10,920 --> 00:14:14,660
It's an especially thick ground fog called Tule Fog.

188
00:14:16,720 --> 00:14:26,300
You can imagine the havoc it sometimes triggers on two of the state's straightest, busiest routes that pass through the region, Interstate 5 and Highway 99.

189
00:14:26,301 --> 00:14:30,480
If you were to drive into a thick bank of Thule fog,

190
00:14:31,260 --> 00:14:34,480
you wouldn't be able to see that fence.

191
00:14:35,780 --> 00:14:39,120
You probably wouldn't be able to see 10 or 12 feet,

192
00:14:39,500 --> 00:14:41,600
and at 65 or 70 miles an hour,

193
00:14:42,180 --> 00:14:45,600
if there's somebody stopped in that fog in the lane that you're in,

194
00:14:46,180 --> 00:14:49,720
if you're not slowing down, that's the recipe for disaster.

195
00:14:49,721 --> 00:14:59,780
California's Tule fog has triggered several massive and deadly freeway pileups,

196
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:03,580
including one of the biggest on November 3, 2007.

197
00:15:05,500 --> 00:15:12,400
On northbound 99, the disastrous sequence of events was set into motion at approximately 8 a.m.

198
00:15:12,600 --> 00:15:19,060
The first car that drove into the fog bank, drove into it at regular freeway speeds, probably 70 miles an hour.

199
00:15:19,720 --> 00:15:23,120
Didn't realize that it was going to be as dense or as thick as it was.

200
00:15:23,620 --> 00:15:25,580
He didn't slow down until he was in it.

201
00:15:27,900 --> 00:15:35,040
After entering the dense fog, the car virtually disappeared from the sight of the driver of an 18-wheeler following close behind.

202
00:15:35,760 --> 00:15:37,700
He never saw the car slow down.

203
00:15:41,040 --> 00:15:47,720
The initial collision set off a chain reaction of accidents as vehicle after vehicle entered the fog bank and crashed.

204
00:15:49,720 --> 00:15:54,720
There was debris and vehicles scattered from this point,

205
00:15:54,721 --> 00:15:57,720
going back this way, approximately one mile.

206
00:15:57,721 --> 00:16:00,720
There were three different clusters involved in it,

207
00:16:00,721 --> 00:16:06,720
102 to 103 vehicles with 18 big rigs involved.

208
00:16:15,720 --> 00:16:18,720
It actually kind of reminded me of some of the footage

209
00:16:18,721 --> 00:16:22,220
of the footage from Desert Storm, the highway to Baghdad,

210
00:16:22,221 --> 00:16:25,220
where they had just bombed out all the vehicles.

211
00:16:25,221 --> 00:16:28,620
The driver of the first car into the fog survived,

212
00:16:28,621 --> 00:16:31,820
but two other motorists died and 39 were injured.

213
00:16:34,820 --> 00:16:37,920
An unfortunate phenomenon makes fog-related traffic pileups

214
00:16:37,921 --> 00:16:40,620
of this magnitude possible.

215
00:16:40,621 --> 00:16:43,420
Many drivers don't instinctively slow down,

216
00:16:43,421 --> 00:16:47,320
even when the fog limits their visibility to near zero.

217
00:16:47,321 --> 00:16:50,420
Seeing no fixed points of reference along the roadside

218
00:16:50,421 --> 00:16:53,160
can make them less aware of their speed.

219
00:16:53,161 --> 00:16:56,100
There's a theory that people are actually speeding up

220
00:16:56,101 --> 00:16:57,500
when they can't see their surroundings

221
00:16:57,501 --> 00:17:01,260
and they can't see how fast they're going.

222
00:17:01,261 --> 00:17:04,340
California's Department of Transportation, Caltrans,

223
00:17:04,341 --> 00:17:05,780
is trying to reduce the danger.

224
00:17:14,640 --> 00:17:17,040
Weather stations and electronic changeable message

225
00:17:17,041 --> 00:17:22,920
signs help motorists avoid fog pileups by alerting them of conditions and advising caution.

226
00:17:24,460 --> 00:17:29,220
But an automated system is only as good as the people driving it want to obey it.

227
00:17:29,900 --> 00:17:33,740
We've got to get the people to realize that when they can't see, they've got to slow down.

228
00:17:33,741 --> 00:17:46,460
Snow causes an average of 150,000 traffic accidents

229
00:17:46,461 --> 00:17:50,160
and kills over 1,300 motorists in the U.S. each year.

230
00:17:52,240 --> 00:17:54,900
Winding through 30 miles of the Colorado Rockies

231
00:17:54,901 --> 00:17:57,880
is one of America's most treacherous snowy roads.

232
00:17:58,080 --> 00:18:01,400
We're standing next to U.S. 550, which is a Colorado highway

233
00:18:01,401 --> 00:18:05,120
that generally runs north and south between Urey and Silverton, Colorado.

234
00:18:06,920 --> 00:18:12,900
Partially because it was built on the same route as a 19th century wagon trail leading to Colorado's silver mines,

235
00:18:13,620 --> 00:18:18,360
this section of U.S. 550 is also referred to as the Million Dollar Highway.

236
00:18:20,600 --> 00:18:27,960
And every winter, nature blankets it with a treasure of snow, 400 inches in an average year.

237
00:18:27,961 --> 00:18:34,960
Average storms an inch an hour, but we can get up to six an hour with a lot of wind,

238
00:18:34,961 --> 00:18:36,960
which causes poor visibility.

239
00:18:36,961 --> 00:18:41,960
Poor visibility and slick, icy surfaces can make a lethal combination.

240
00:18:41,961 --> 00:18:47,960
It's worse on the million-dollar highway because the road is narrow,

241
00:18:47,961 --> 00:18:52,960
lacks guardrails, and has drops of up to 400 feet.

242
00:18:52,961 --> 00:18:56,960
There's some very steep drop-offs here, and we don't have a place to anchor the guardrail adequately.

243
00:18:56,961 --> 00:19:01,260
adequately, and it helps not to have the guardrail to be able to clear the snow off the highway.

244
00:19:02,520 --> 00:19:07,820
But drivers making this dangerous drive have to worry about more than just snow falling from the sky.

245
00:19:08,700 --> 00:19:13,420
The Million Dollar Highway is one of the most avalanche-prone roads in the United States.

246
00:19:14,380 --> 00:19:19,460
In the 25-mile stretch between Oray and Silverton, and then south towards Durango,

247
00:19:19,461 --> 00:19:26,140
there's 70 known avalanche paths, making the highway the highest avalanche road per mile in North America.

248
00:19:26,960 --> 00:19:31,800
By far the highest hazards on these passes during the wintertime are the avalanche hazards.

249
00:19:36,400 --> 00:19:39,640
We average hundreds of avalanches along this road every year,

250
00:19:40,200 --> 00:19:43,820
and unfortunately we've had people killed just up the road from us here.

251
00:19:44,140 --> 00:19:48,180
On occasion, there's so much snow up here and so many avalanches that we've either set off

252
00:19:48,181 --> 00:19:51,120
or that have come down naturally that we have to close the road to all traffic.

253
00:19:53,220 --> 00:19:56,700
But whether the snow on the road comes from avalanches or storms,

254
00:19:56,960 --> 00:20:04,740
The Colorado Department of Transportation's basic countermeasure to the danger is snow removal.

255
00:20:06,280 --> 00:20:10,580
And not surprisingly, this prodigious task carries its own dangers.

256
00:20:12,440 --> 00:20:16,440
Over the last few decades, five workers have lost their lives.

257
00:20:18,520 --> 00:20:25,220
When these guys are out plowing the snow off the highway, the dangers from the avalanche,

258
00:20:25,760 --> 00:20:29,340
You just, you never know when they're going to, when the slides are going to run.

259
00:20:30,860 --> 00:20:34,380
For these workers, as well as the motorists who drive here in the winter,

260
00:20:34,800 --> 00:20:37,060
the best defense against danger is caution.

261
00:20:37,580 --> 00:20:40,860
You need to be very careful in just driving slow and watching the speed limits

262
00:20:40,861 --> 00:20:42,340
and be careful around all the curves.

263
00:20:42,580 --> 00:20:44,020
There's really no second chances up here.

264
00:20:45,320 --> 00:20:50,820
One American city has taken great strides improving our chances on roads made dangerous by weather.

265
00:20:51,880 --> 00:20:53,020
Houston, Texas.

266
00:20:55,220 --> 00:20:57,660
Here, they are no stranger to extreme weather events.

267
00:20:59,100 --> 00:21:03,100
In 2001, Tropical Storm Allison flooded most of the city.

268
00:21:04,560 --> 00:21:08,840
In 2005, Hurricane Rita forced a mass evacuation.

269
00:21:10,220 --> 00:21:15,180
And in 2017, Hurricane Harvey caused more than 100 deaths.

270
00:21:16,180 --> 00:21:18,880
But to help cut down on these catastrophes,

271
00:21:19,940 --> 00:21:23,080
Houston operates a special monitoring and communications facility

272
00:21:23,081 --> 00:21:27,480
called Houston Transtar that helps safeguard its motorists.

273
00:21:28,180 --> 00:21:30,480
It also acts as an emergency management center.

274
00:21:30,920 --> 00:21:34,320
We monitor over 600 cameras on the freeways.

275
00:21:34,760 --> 00:21:39,280
We have the four main screens here where we watch tours of the major freeways.

276
00:21:39,281 --> 00:21:45,220
We have over 300 miles of fiber out there where we're bringing back the information from sensors.

277
00:21:46,180 --> 00:21:48,420
This is our typical weather sensor.

278
00:21:48,800 --> 00:21:50,580
It can be configured in different ways.

279
00:21:50,581 --> 00:21:55,300
For roadway flooding, it has a rain gauge and a pressure transducer.

280
00:21:56,800 --> 00:22:03,080
We have wind gauges, which measure wind speed, wind direction, and also peak wind gusts.

281
00:22:03,940 --> 00:22:09,680
They also have humidity, barometric pressure, and that's mainly for hurricanes and tropical storms.

282
00:22:10,780 --> 00:22:13,500
Once the sensors collect the data and analyze it,

283
00:22:14,200 --> 00:22:17,140
TransStar relays it to the public via its official website.

284
00:22:17,141 --> 00:22:22,480
email alerts to registered users and dynamic message signs along highways.

285
00:22:24,400 --> 00:22:28,240
Whether it's torrential downpours, heavy snow or dense fog,

286
00:22:29,240 --> 00:22:32,540
there is one universal recommendation from the transportation pros

287
00:22:32,541 --> 00:22:34,720
when it comes to driving in those conditions.

288
00:22:36,740 --> 00:22:39,120
If you don't need to go, don't go.

289
00:22:39,500 --> 00:22:42,260
The best bet is you can avoid that trip, stay at home.

290
00:22:42,261 --> 00:22:45,640
But if you have to get out in it, you need to be cautious,

291
00:22:45,641 --> 00:22:50,340
slow your speeds down, have greater following distances,

292
00:22:50,620 --> 00:22:53,120
and just be aware of the conditions at all times.

293
00:22:53,400 --> 00:22:55,000
Even when the weather is fine,

294
00:22:55,480 --> 00:22:58,160
a leisure drive can turn dangerous in an instant.

295
00:23:00,660 --> 00:23:04,760
On average, dangerous roads in the U.S. take 100 lives every day.

296
00:23:11,060 --> 00:23:14,400
Making for some of the most dangerous drives in the United States

297
00:23:14,401 --> 00:23:18,220
are without a doubt two-lane and multi-lane highways.

298
00:23:19,360 --> 00:23:20,620
Let me get you to come on over here, man.

299
00:23:21,700 --> 00:23:24,560
And no one knows that better than the officers that patrol them.

300
00:23:25,060 --> 00:23:27,480
If you're up here and anybody hits the back of my patrol car,

301
00:23:28,100 --> 00:23:31,280
there's a good chance you're going to get caught somewhere in between that car and your car.

302
00:23:32,900 --> 00:23:34,320
I don't want that to happen to you.

303
00:23:38,880 --> 00:23:41,340
Just like that, see? Just like that.

304
00:23:41,341 --> 00:23:48,920
An average of 38,000 people perish on America's 4 million miles of highways each year,

305
00:23:49,900 --> 00:23:53,160
comprising 90% of the nation's total transportation deaths.

306
00:23:57,740 --> 00:24:00,311
Even so, highway fatality rates in the U.S.

307
00:24:00,312 --> 00:24:03,480
have dropped significantly over the past few decades.

308
00:24:04,580 --> 00:24:10,020
In 2019, it was just 1.1 fatalities per 100 million miles driven.

309
00:24:11,340 --> 00:24:13,940
one of the lowest fatality rates in history.

310
00:24:15,820 --> 00:24:19,940
This fatality rate is minuscule compared to the rates in the early 20th century.

311
00:24:21,280 --> 00:24:24,160
The era witnessed its peak in 1909,

312
00:24:24,580 --> 00:24:29,120
when there were 45 fatalities per 100 miles driven,

313
00:24:30,780 --> 00:24:35,720
an astronomical 40 times greater than today.

314
00:24:35,721 --> 00:24:36,815
The reason?

315
00:24:36,816 --> 00:24:44,680
Thousands of new inexperienced drivers in cars lacking today's safety features,

316
00:24:45,200 --> 00:24:47,462
navigating primitive roads.

317
00:24:47,463 --> 00:24:52,740
But the early 20th century also witnessed the advent of highway

318
00:24:52,741 --> 00:24:58,220
hardware, such as median barriers, guardrails, signposts, and lane markers,

319
00:24:58,660 --> 00:25:01,820
that revolutionized road safety.

320
00:25:01,821 --> 00:25:07,820
The roadside hardware has been around pretty much since the roadway system was developed,

321
00:25:07,821 --> 00:25:12,820
but we've been able to make roadside hardware better and better over the years by investing

322
00:25:12,821 --> 00:25:16,820
in research and by investing in technology.

323
00:25:16,821 --> 00:25:25,820
The Federal Highway Administration helps test and develop highway hardware.

324
00:25:25,821 --> 00:25:39,184
Since the late 1980s, FHWA engineers have crushed cars, battered barriers, and smashed guardrails at the Federal Outdoor Impact Laboratory, or FOIL, in McLean,

325
00:25:39,185 --> 00:25:39,940
Virginia.

326
00:25:44,460 --> 00:25:46,380
All in the name of road safety.

327
00:25:49,820 --> 00:25:53,840
Their test results are then studied by the National Crash Analysis Center.

328
00:25:55,820 --> 00:25:59,820
They used to do all their crash testing this way,

329
00:25:59,821 --> 00:26:03,820
but now they rely heavily on computerized simulations

330
00:26:03,821 --> 00:26:07,820
using virtual models of cars and highway hardware.

331
00:26:07,821 --> 00:26:15,820
They now use real-world tests to confirm their virtual models behave like the real McCoy.

332
00:26:15,821 --> 00:26:19,820
This track is one of the two major impact facilities we have here

333
00:26:19,821 --> 00:26:23,820
at the Federal Outdoor Impact Laboratory.

334
00:26:23,821 --> 00:26:30,540
Behind me you see our accelerator that allows us to do full-scale crash testing over a range

335
00:26:30,541 --> 00:26:34,720
of vehicles ranging from small cars to single unit trucks.

336
00:26:34,721 --> 00:26:39,080
We can conduct the tests at various impact speeds and we can repeat that time and time

337
00:26:39,081 --> 00:26:41,380
again.

338
00:26:41,381 --> 00:26:48,460
To test how some highway hardware withstands impacts, engineers use a massive slab of concrete

339
00:26:48,461 --> 00:26:51,340
instead of an actual vehicle.

340
00:26:51,341 --> 00:26:53,400
Behind me we have the pendulum test.

341
00:26:53,401 --> 00:26:59,840
The 5,000 pound concrete block that's there now is basically representing a pickup truck

342
00:26:59,841 --> 00:27:11,380
which when released will hit the guardrail at 25 miles an hour.

343
00:27:11,381 --> 00:27:15,520
This test is conducted to determine how much damage guardrails can take before they can

344
00:27:15,521 --> 00:27:16,960
no longer serve their purpose.

345
00:27:21,340 --> 00:27:31,260
That purpose is to absorb the energy and reduce the speed of any vehicle that hits the guardrail,

346
00:27:31,700 --> 00:27:35,980
while also keeping it on the shoulder instead of diverting it back into traffic lanes.

347
00:27:42,800 --> 00:27:49,460
The main purpose of highway medians is to provide separation between opposite directions of travel.

348
00:27:49,461 --> 00:27:55,220
But some states like North Carolina and Washington have had chronic problems with cars crossing

349
00:27:55,221 --> 00:28:00,160
medians without barriers and then slamming into oncoming traffic.

350
00:28:00,161 --> 00:28:06,300
One solution widely implemented in the 1990s was a crucial highway hardware addition, the

351
00:28:06,301 --> 00:28:10,340
cable median barrier.

352
00:28:10,341 --> 00:28:15,140
These cables mounted on posts not only help prevent vehicles from breaking through, but

353
00:28:15,141 --> 00:28:20,860
also function like a guardrail, preventing cars from bouncing back into harm's way in traffic

354
00:28:20,861 --> 00:28:24,128
lanes. Its impact has been monumental.

355
00:28:24,129 --> 00:28:28,600
In some cases, it has been 90% effective in reducing

356
00:28:28,601 --> 00:28:33,860
the incidence of head-on collisions. But the strategic placement of highway hardware

357
00:28:33,861 --> 00:28:38,593
is just as vital to countering danger as its design.

358
00:28:38,594 --> 00:28:41,960
This test car dips beneath the lowest

359
00:28:41,961 --> 00:28:47,360
cable of the barrier placed on the far side of a ditch.

360
00:28:47,361 --> 00:28:54,880
By moving the barrier to the near side of the ditch, the cable performs as it should.

361
00:28:54,881 --> 00:28:59,020
Even with these safety improvements, many United States highways are still becoming

362
00:28:59,021 --> 00:29:02,020
antiquated.

363
00:29:02,021 --> 00:29:06,500
Highways and roadways in some of our rural areas, they haven't changed a lot over the

364
00:29:06,501 --> 00:29:09,740
last 50 years, 60 years, 70 years.

365
00:29:09,741 --> 00:29:14,540
They're still very windy, they're still very narrow.

366
00:29:14,541 --> 00:29:19,400
And they're often plagued by trees, telephone poles and other fixtures set dangerously close

367
00:29:19,401 --> 00:29:24,140
to the shoulders.

368
00:29:24,141 --> 00:29:28,320
But the Federal Highway Administration has engineered to evaluate the perils of these

369
00:29:28,321 --> 00:29:35,620
and other dangerous roads with a highway simulator.

370
00:29:35,621 --> 00:29:43,160
sit inside a full-sized car and view an 8-foot-high display with a wraparound 270-degree perspective.

371
00:29:43,161 --> 00:29:47,460
Digitally recorded images of actual roads are visible through the windshield and all

372
00:29:47,461 --> 00:29:50,660
door windows.

373
00:29:50,661 --> 00:29:56,240
And strategically placed monitors act as side view and rear view mirrors, creating a realistic

374
00:29:56,241 --> 00:29:58,240
driving experience.

375
00:29:58,241 --> 00:29:59,240
And it's interactive.

376
00:29:59,241 --> 00:30:02,100
So when they turn, the scenario turns.

377
00:30:02,101 --> 00:30:03,860
When they stop, the scenario stops.

378
00:30:03,861 --> 00:30:06,080
If they speed up, the scenario speeds up.

379
00:30:06,960 --> 00:30:13,040
The simulator's motion base creates the sensation of accelerating, turning, and climbing in descending hills.

380
00:30:14,300 --> 00:30:20,320
The goal is to determine how drivers respond to hazards on dangerous roads in a safe, controlled environment.

381
00:30:21,740 --> 00:30:27,600
Studying their reactions and habits allows engineers to assess a road's design flaws and correct them.

382
00:30:28,060 --> 00:30:32,940
But the simulator also allows engineers to analyze roads yet to be constructed.

383
00:30:33,860 --> 00:30:37,200
Once trouble spots are identified, it's back to the drawing board.

384
00:30:40,280 --> 00:30:45,220
Statistically, the most dangerous roads on Earth are in third world and developing nations.

385
00:30:46,800 --> 00:30:50,820
While China and the United States are near the top of the most dangerous roads list,

386
00:30:51,800 --> 00:30:55,700
today no country suffers more traffic fatalities than India.

387
00:30:56,900 --> 00:31:01,640
India accounts for more than 85% of all traffic deaths a year worldwide.

388
00:31:01,641 --> 00:31:06,180
that means that 151,000 Indians die each year,

389
00:31:07,220 --> 00:31:09,640
four times as many as in the United States.

390
00:31:13,520 --> 00:31:17,160
Roads in the developing world and third world countries

391
00:31:17,161 --> 00:31:20,220
have a tendency to be more hazardous than our roads here,

392
00:31:20,720 --> 00:31:23,480
primarily because we have been at it for a while now

393
00:31:23,481 --> 00:31:27,440
and have worked to make roads safe for the vehicles that are on them

394
00:31:27,441 --> 00:31:29,880
in developing countries, third world countries.

395
00:31:29,881 --> 00:31:32,880
It's a new concept.

396
00:31:32,881 --> 00:31:38,880
The main issues are these roadways have all different variety of traffic on the same road.

397
00:31:38,881 --> 00:31:44,880
Like you have passenger vehicles, you have trucks, you have pedestrians, you have rickshaws, you have cycles,

398
00:31:44,881 --> 00:31:49,880
which actually reduces the capacity and adds the safety concern.

399
00:31:51,880 --> 00:31:57,880
Compounding the danger is that hundreds of thousands of new drivers take to these cluttered roads every year.

400
00:31:57,881 --> 00:32:01,940
from a generation with virtually no car culture heritage.

401
00:32:03,100 --> 00:32:05,020
In the U.S. and in Western Europe,

402
00:32:05,560 --> 00:32:08,420
you've had generations that have operated vehicles,

403
00:32:08,660 --> 00:32:11,700
so people have almost a cultural instinct

404
00:32:11,701 --> 00:32:14,400
on what you do when you get behind the wheel of a car.

405
00:32:16,060 --> 00:32:17,300
Not so in China,

406
00:32:17,860 --> 00:32:19,580
where millions of new drivers

407
00:32:19,581 --> 00:32:21,940
are turning the nation's growing interstate system

408
00:32:21,941 --> 00:32:23,340
into a crapshoot.

409
00:32:23,860 --> 00:32:27,380
Today, the Chinese, driving along their new superhighways,

410
00:32:27,381 --> 00:32:29,980
are high-speed neophytes.

411
00:32:30,220 --> 00:32:31,420
They don't know what they're doing.

412
00:32:31,500 --> 00:32:33,400
They're moving about. They're changing lanes.

413
00:32:33,480 --> 00:32:35,840
They're using their horns when people can't possibly hear them.

414
00:32:36,180 --> 00:32:37,120
It is chaotic.

415
00:32:38,160 --> 00:32:40,000
Traveling on the Chinese interstates today

416
00:32:40,001 --> 00:32:41,760
is like being in the Wild West.

417
00:32:42,060 --> 00:32:42,960
Anything goes.

418
00:32:44,740 --> 00:32:47,780
Beyond improving the education and training of its drivers,

419
00:32:48,320 --> 00:32:51,220
developing nations are enlisting the help of traffic specialists,

420
00:32:51,780 --> 00:32:54,560
like those at Arcady's in Atlanta, Georgia.

421
00:32:57,380 --> 00:33:03,705
They design safe, smoothly flowing roads based on successful systems developed over decades in the U.S.

422
00:33:03,706 --> 00:33:04,406
and Europe.

423
00:33:05,600 --> 00:33:17,440
We regularly develop simulation models which always become lifelike in being able to look at the roadway on a computer as we currently have designed it.

424
00:33:17,800 --> 00:33:21,540
We can create three-dimensional models that we can communicate with our client.

425
00:33:21,541 --> 00:33:27,920
These models allow engineers to dissect the flaws of existing roads or highways

426
00:33:27,921 --> 00:33:31,060
and determine how they can refigure them to improve traffic flow.

427
00:33:33,860 --> 00:33:37,700
A major focus of companies like Arcadis is urban intersections.

428
00:33:39,480 --> 00:33:42,220
Globally, most traffic accidents occur in intersections,

429
00:33:42,780 --> 00:33:46,880
and design flaws of intersections in developing nations multiply the dangers.

430
00:33:46,881 --> 00:33:56,400
Many have no traffic signals, marked traffic lanes, sidewalks or crosswalks, creating gridlock and chaos.

431
00:33:56,900 --> 00:34:02,100
There is already so much congestion that everybody wants to get to their destination as soon as possible,

432
00:34:02,101 --> 00:34:05,060
so they kind of get frustrated and break the rules.

433
00:34:05,760 --> 00:34:08,900
Case in point, this impatient bus driver in China.

434
00:34:09,860 --> 00:34:13,420
The traffic signal is red, and there are several cars stopped in front of him.

435
00:34:13,421 --> 00:34:18,420
So he turns intentionally into oncoming traffic lanes and runs the light.

436
00:34:18,421 --> 00:34:20,420
Only luck prevents a collision.

437
00:34:20,421 --> 00:34:25,420
Can traffic engineers discourage stunts like this with effective intersection design?

438
00:34:25,421 --> 00:34:30,420
As engineers, we try to lay out the road to properly convey to the traveling public,

439
00:34:30,421 --> 00:34:34,420
through signing and marking, what is approaching them at the intersection.

440
00:34:34,421 --> 00:34:39,420
Proper edge pavement delineation, right turn arrows, left turn arrows,

441
00:34:39,421 --> 00:34:43,140
to delineate which lanes are through lanes, which lanes are left-turn lanes,

442
00:34:43,420 --> 00:34:44,700
which lanes are right-turn lanes,

443
00:34:44,820 --> 00:34:49,680
and then those are sequenced with the signal phases at the main intersection.

444
00:34:52,480 --> 00:34:56,900
Adding well-coordinated signal systems at problematic intersections like this one

445
00:34:56,901 --> 00:35:01,220
not only help mitigate the danger to drivers, but also to pedestrians.

446
00:35:03,720 --> 00:35:06,620
Deficient intersection design encourages jaywalking.

447
00:35:09,420 --> 00:35:13,820
and half of all traffic fatalities in developing nations involve pedestrians.

448
00:35:15,500 --> 00:35:20,060
In addition to the roadway design for vehicular traffic, we also try to put in elements of

449
00:35:20,061 --> 00:35:24,007
pedestrian access, like the sidewalk that I'm standing on now.

450
00:35:24,008 --> 00:35:25,980
We'll provide proper pedestrian

451
00:35:25,981 --> 00:35:29,342
crosswalks and signalized free-flowing movements for pedestrians.

452
00:35:29,343 --> 00:35:31,100
They'll get their own signal phase

453
00:35:31,101 --> 00:35:31,801
as well.

454
00:35:31,802 --> 00:35:35,740
So that can safely and efficiently move the pedestrians safely through an intersection

455
00:35:35,741 --> 00:35:36,740
and avoiding conflict.

456
00:35:38,360 --> 00:35:41,280
Maybe the most unusual kind of dangerous drive

457
00:35:41,281 --> 00:35:44,720
is created by drivers who go to great lengths to be on them.

458
00:35:57,040 --> 00:35:59,680
A special breed of competitors like this bunch

459
00:35:59,681 --> 00:36:01,080
outside of Cedar City, Utah,

460
00:36:01,500 --> 00:36:04,640
is pushing the boundaries of off-roading to new heights.

461
00:36:05,740 --> 00:36:19,740
They relish the challenge of maneuvering highly modified trucks or buggies over terrain that most casual off-roaders would consider impossible to navigate.

462
00:36:27,020 --> 00:36:30,500
Their sport, aptly enough, is called rock crawling.

463
00:36:32,420 --> 00:36:35,180
It feels like a roller coaster every single course.

464
00:36:35,740 --> 00:36:37,940
You know, a lot of new people come out and watch,

465
00:36:38,000 --> 00:36:39,300
and they think, man, this is insane.

466
00:36:39,360 --> 00:36:40,420
I can't believe you guys do this.

467
00:36:40,600 --> 00:36:42,320
One thing that all these guys have in common

468
00:36:42,321 --> 00:36:46,120
is just going on a typical trail ride

469
00:36:46,121 --> 00:36:47,820
isn't quite enough for them.

470
00:36:47,860 --> 00:36:49,900
They want to be pushed to do something extreme,

471
00:36:50,120 --> 00:36:52,220
not just go out and do the same thing everyone else is.

472
00:36:54,620 --> 00:36:56,840
And you start feeling comfortable

473
00:36:56,841 --> 00:37:00,000
being at 70-degree angles up or down

474
00:37:00,001 --> 00:37:02,120
and 50-degree angles sideways.

475
00:37:02,640 --> 00:37:04,120
The competition is different

476
00:37:04,121 --> 00:37:06,720
because it's usually a rock pile someplace,

477
00:37:06,721 --> 00:37:10,360
and we're just trying to drive up the most extreme thing

478
00:37:10,361 --> 00:37:12,100
that the course designer can think of.

479
00:37:16,760 --> 00:37:19,800
Nicole Johnson has been driving rock crawlers

480
00:37:19,801 --> 00:37:21,500
competitively since 2001.

481
00:37:22,080 --> 00:37:24,000
I think it's really exciting, actually,

482
00:37:24,001 --> 00:37:25,740
to be behind the wheel of this car.

483
00:37:26,140 --> 00:37:27,440
When you're driving up a big rock,

484
00:37:27,500 --> 00:37:29,040
sometimes all you see is sky and clouds.

485
00:37:29,560 --> 00:37:31,140
When you're coming off of something steep,

486
00:37:31,220 --> 00:37:33,920
you just see ground, and you just hope you can run it out

487
00:37:33,921 --> 00:37:34,621
Not rolling.

488
00:37:36,260 --> 00:37:37,320
Okay, drive forward.

489
00:37:37,860 --> 00:37:38,560
Three wheels.

490
00:37:38,561 --> 00:37:40,320
Nicole, like every driver in rock crawling,

491
00:37:40,760 --> 00:37:43,560
depends on a spotter to act as a second set of eyes

492
00:37:43,561 --> 00:37:45,000
and direct the overall effort.

493
00:37:45,360 --> 00:37:46,800
Go up over there. We're backing down.

494
00:37:47,160 --> 00:37:49,600
Nicole's spotter is her husband, Frank.

495
00:37:49,960 --> 00:37:53,440
There's just little cues that you look for in the suspension

496
00:37:53,441 --> 00:37:56,840
and the way the car's built that you can kind of tell that,

497
00:37:56,940 --> 00:37:58,940
okay, we got it, or now we got to back off.

498
00:37:59,440 --> 00:38:00,140
Steer me.

499
00:38:00,141 --> 00:38:00,841
Okay, stop.

500
00:38:01,080 --> 00:38:02,880
The driver might get all of the glory,

501
00:38:02,881 --> 00:38:04,760
but it's the spotter that does all the work.

502
00:38:05,160 --> 00:38:07,500
The spotter's the poor guy that's got to run around the vehicle

503
00:38:07,501 --> 00:38:09,460
and pulling on ropes and stacking rocks

504
00:38:09,461 --> 00:38:10,960
and pointing us where we need to go.

505
00:38:14,120 --> 00:38:16,980
The spotters face the greatest dangers in rock crawling.

506
00:38:19,460 --> 00:38:20,820
This one's great, you're well.

507
00:38:23,460 --> 00:38:26,000
Especially in the all-too-likely event of a rollover.

508
00:38:27,940 --> 00:38:31,140
Spotters falling down cliffs and hurting their legs or their knees

509
00:38:31,141 --> 00:38:32,260
or any part of their body, really,

510
00:38:32,261 --> 00:38:36,100
If the spotter's in a bad position, they could get run over.

511
00:38:40,340 --> 00:38:46,140
For Nicole and Frank, the secret to minimizing the danger is trust and good communication.

512
00:38:48,120 --> 00:38:48,940
Drive forward.

513
00:38:49,240 --> 00:38:50,180
It doesn't feel right.

514
00:38:51,020 --> 00:38:51,800
Drive forward.

515
00:38:52,340 --> 00:38:53,040
There you go.

516
00:38:53,041 --> 00:38:53,741
You really have to trust.

517
00:38:53,880 --> 00:38:55,460
Sometimes a car feels really weird.

518
00:38:55,580 --> 00:38:57,460
Feels like you're going to roll when you're really not.

519
00:38:57,500 --> 00:38:58,900
And he can tell if it's going to.

520
00:38:59,080 --> 00:38:59,820
Rear dig down.

521
00:38:59,920 --> 00:39:00,620
Rear dig down.

522
00:39:00,621 --> 00:39:01,321
Down this way.

523
00:39:01,322 --> 00:39:02,022
Yeah.

524
00:39:02,023 --> 00:39:03,700
Your ass hand has to be that way, Nicole.

525
00:39:04,500 --> 00:39:05,620
I'm trying, Frank.

526
00:39:05,900 --> 00:39:08,680
Our way of dealing with it, maybe we yell at each other.

527
00:39:08,980 --> 00:39:10,760
Work left. Go left now. Go left.

528
00:39:11,020 --> 00:39:12,580
All the way left. Work it left.

529
00:39:12,720 --> 00:39:13,440
That's right.

530
00:39:13,760 --> 00:39:15,340
Go right. Whatever that is.

531
00:39:15,460 --> 00:39:18,960
But it's all, you know, water under the bridge at that point.

532
00:39:19,040 --> 00:39:20,740
We're here to have fun, and that's what it about.

533
00:39:20,820 --> 00:39:23,920
We had a lot of fights today, but, you know, we're smiling.

534
00:39:25,840 --> 00:39:26,580
It's all good.

535
00:39:26,740 --> 00:39:27,440
It's good.

536
00:39:27,441 --> 00:39:34,220
Beyond good teamwork, competitors counter the sport's inherent dangers with technology.

537
00:39:34,221 --> 00:39:35,893
Your dangers are really high.

538
00:39:35,894 --> 00:39:44,547
If you're not wearing your proper safety harnesses, if you're not wearing your helmet or fire suits or anything like that, the danger is always there.

539
00:39:44,548 --> 00:39:46,220
It's any kind of motor sport.

540
00:39:46,221 --> 00:39:53,140
Rock crawling is a very dangerous sport.

541
00:39:53,141 --> 00:39:55,160
We do treat it with respect.

542
00:39:55,161 --> 00:40:00,000
We make sure that we have the best of the best as far as a suspension seat, the strongest

543
00:40:00,001 --> 00:40:03,380
seat belts that we can get.

544
00:40:03,381 --> 00:40:10,080
And these modified monsters also sport specialized tires available only for competitors, tailor-made

545
00:40:10,081 --> 00:40:12,000
for the task.

546
00:40:12,001 --> 00:40:13,440
These are not your normal street tires.

547
00:40:13,441 --> 00:40:18,480
These are 37-inch Maxxis trepidors, and as you can see just by the tread, they're ultra-sticky,

548
00:40:18,481 --> 00:40:22,900
and this is what keeps us clinging to those rocks on those side hills, on those big climbs.

549
00:40:22,901 --> 00:40:25,440
This is what gets us up there.

550
00:40:25,441 --> 00:40:32,800
But it's more than just enormous tires that get these buggies around these rocks.

551
00:40:32,801 --> 00:40:39,940
It also takes state-of-the-art suspension systems.

552
00:40:39,941 --> 00:40:42,320
The suspension is known as a four-lane.

553
00:40:42,321 --> 00:40:44,120
As you can see, there are two lower trailing arms

554
00:40:44,121 --> 00:40:45,260
and two upper trailing arms.

555
00:40:45,500 --> 00:40:46,940
Those upper trailing arms are actually

556
00:40:46,941 --> 00:40:48,800
what locate the axle underneath the vehicle,

557
00:40:49,140 --> 00:40:51,540
and then the lowers simply provide support.

558
00:40:52,300 --> 00:40:55,520
This particular suspension gives us great movement up and down

559
00:40:55,521 --> 00:40:58,740
for wheel travel and really helps us cling to those hard side

560
00:40:58,741 --> 00:41:00,500
hills and those nasty crawling sections.

561
00:41:02,260 --> 00:41:05,580
And specialized brake controls allow independent operation

562
00:41:05,581 --> 00:41:06,940
of the front and rear brakes.

563
00:41:09,080 --> 00:41:11,860
While most of us do the best we can to avoid danger

564
00:41:11,861 --> 00:41:15,520
when we're behind the wheel, these thrill seekers pursue it

565
00:41:15,521 --> 00:41:18,100
and savor its taste.

566
00:41:19,440 --> 00:41:22,480
When you're hanging off these cliffs and you're, you know,

567
00:41:22,520 --> 00:41:24,360
looking down ready to roll a vehicle,

568
00:41:25,220 --> 00:41:28,020
or you're leaning sideways and having to, you know,

569
00:41:28,080 --> 00:41:30,200
pull an awesome save, trust me,

570
00:41:30,260 --> 00:41:32,740
there's a ton of adrenaline going through your body.

571
00:41:33,000 --> 00:41:35,220
I think you have to be a little bit of an adrenaline junkie,

572
00:41:35,760 --> 00:41:39,680
and I can't think of any other sport that I would rather do.

573
00:41:39,681 --> 00:41:48,820
For these extreme athletes, the more clear and present danger may lurk where they rejoin the rest of us, on the established roads.

574
00:41:52,780 --> 00:41:55,200
No need to seek out danger here.

575
00:41:56,400 --> 00:41:57,100
Sooner or later.

576
00:42:00,820 --> 00:42:03,460
Just like that, see? Just like that.

